Module pellam "The Liberty of the Ice" {
  // ── Endings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Reached on the morning after the ice goes out, by what the three days prepared. The first that holds is the one
  // told; what became of each of the others (the alderman, the printer, the heretic, the debtor, the bear, Ursel) is
  // told before it, as the morning's news (aftermath.kyr). None of them is a custom kept for ever after.
  Ending pillory "The Pillory" when fourthDay() and pilloried(party) "You come off the ice at nine at night with snow in your hair and your hands numb from the pillory's holes, and the Lisk goes out behind you at eleven, and in the morning it is a river, and nobody in Pellam remembers your face except as the Squire of Hawsfold, which is a joke now in every taproom on both banks. You leave by the North Road with what you came with, less what the pillory cost you, and on the North Bar someone has chalked a sheep with a crown on. Justice Broom, they tell you, said afterwards that it was the best lie of the Fair, and she was sorry about the pillory, and would do it again.".
  Ending sold "Five Coin a Head" when fourthDay() and drownedSold(party) "In the morning the river is running and Harker's hired are on Ossett's agent's sledges on the south road over the downs, roped, the first party out of Pellam since the snow, and among them, coughing, Dickon Cobb, landed. You have fifteen coin. Kemble wrote you a receipt. Widow Saffery's rope-walk house on the bridge is shut and nobody knows where she is. On the stake by the Black Reach, where the hole was, somebody has hung a hat, and under it, in the snow, put a penny.".
  Ending unswearing "The Unswearing" when fourthDay() and unsworn(lisk) "In the morning there is a river under Pellam Bridge, and in the Court's book, landed with everything else before the ice went, a new line in the Reeve's own hand: A HAND IS BOUND TO WHAT THE ONE WHO SHOOK IT KNEW. It is only law on the river, and the river is water now; but it was cried at the Glove Post before the whole of Pellam, and by noon the Wool Exchange is in a panic, because if it were law on dry ground half the bargains in the city would be void, and every factor on the floor knows which half. Harker's hired walk out of Pellam by the North Road in the thaw, north to the fells, free. Master Harker does not come to the Exchange. Edrin Callow, discharged, preaches in Bankside that nothing stays, except, he now adds, what a person truly knew; and has more listeners than he did, and coughs less.".
  Ending long_con "The Long Con" when fourthDay() and hiredReleased(lisk) and hiredSquire(lisk) "In the morning there is a river, and twenty young people from the north country are walking up the North Road in the thaw toward the fells, free, with their tokens still in their hats, because Master Josias Harker, wool-factor and churchwarden, sold their hands to the Squire of Hawsfold for two hundred ewes, and the Squire gave them back before the Reeve. Harker has a landed bargain for a flock at the shearing, good in any court of the country, and in the spring he will go up to Hawsfold under Swale Fell with it to collect. What he finds there depends on Abel Haws. The Fair is already a ballad on Noll Brisket's press, in which the Squire has a golden fleece and Harker is a goose.".
  Ending flight "Not Landed" when fourthDay() and hiredFled(lisk) "In the morning there is a river under Pellam Bridge, and on the far side of the downs, in the snow, twenty people from the north country walking north-about to the fells by roads nobody in Pellam can follow until the thaw comes up the valleys; not landed, and not anybody's. Master Harker's bondhouse on Hay Wharf stands open on the river with its straw trodden and its brazier cold. Kemble's book has twenty little drawn hands in it, for shaken, and against every one, in the Court's book, nothing at all. On the Black Reach the hole that never froze is only river now, like the rest.".
  Ending flock_that_was "The Flock That Was" when fourthDay() and flockHonest(lisk) "In the morning there is a river, and a bargain landed in the Court's book, good from here to the sea: two hundred Swale ewes at Hawsfold under Swale Fell, to Josias Harker, at the shearing. They exist. You bought them off Abel Haws for forty coin and sold them to Harker for sixty, and every hand that was shaken in it will be kept, which is more than can be said for any other bargain of the Fair. Harker will get his ewes, and Abel his forty, and you twenty and a story. It is the most honest swindle anyone in Pellam has ever heard of, and nobody believes it.".
  Ending score_lost "The Score, and Ursel's" when fourthDay() and urselTook(party) and melted(flock_bargain) "In the morning the Lisk is running, and the bargain for two hundred ewes that never were has gone out to sea with it, unlanded, and Master Harker is thirty coin the poorer; and so are you, because Ursel Cobb took it out of your coat at the Glove Post five minutes after he paid it, and went, and never shook on anything. Somewhere on the North Road in the thaw there is a woman in a caped coat with sixty coin, laughing like a door. You find, after a while, that you do not entirely mind.".
  Ending score "The Score" when fourthDay() and earnestTaken(party) and melted(flock_bargain) "In the morning there is a river under Pellam Bridge, and somewhere at the bottom of it, going out to sea, a bargain for two hundred fell ewes at Hawsfold that was shaken on the ice and never landed. Master Harker is thirty coin the poorer and the Squire of Hawsfold is nowhere. In the taprooms of Bankside they are telling it already, with improvements. Of Harker's hired, they say nothing: they were landed at the Landing, and went south on Ossett's agent's sledges at first light, which is the kind of thing nobody tells.".
  Ending bound "Hired" when fourthDay() and hiredHand(party) and landed(hire_bargain) "In the morning there is a river, and you are on Ossett's agent's sledge on the south road over the downs, roped to Tansy Hobb on one side and a big lad with a whip in his hat on the other, landed, a year's service, good in any court of the country, on a farm in the hills that is a hole in a hill called Gallowmoor. You shook. Harker's palm was soft and very warm.".
  Ending away "Away" when fourthDay() and not inPellam() and not earnestTaken(party) "You are not in Pellam when the ice goes out, and you hear of it in another country, from a drover: the Lisk at Pellam froze from bank to bank and they held a Fair on it, the first in thirty-one winters, with a gin-tent and a printing press and an ox, and a law of its own; and it went out on the third night with a noise like the end of the world. Something about a wool-factor and his hired. The drover does not remember, and talks about the price of fleece.".
  Ending landed "Landed" when fourthDay() "In the morning there is a river under Pellam Bridge, black and running, full of ice. Everything that was landed at the Glove Post is good from here to the sea: Harker's twenty hired among the rest, who went south at first light on Ossett's agent's sledges over the downs, roped, with their tokens still in their hats, to the farm in the hills at Gallowmoor. Pellam goes back to the wool. The watermen put their boats in. Noll Brisket prints a ballad of the Great Frost, at a penny, in which nobody drowns.".
}
